  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Dry weather expected through midday, then scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms develop this afternoon and evening.\n\nHigh pressure over the area will keep dry weather intact through \nmidday, with any patchy fog dissipating by mid morning.\n\nHigh pressure will shift east later this morning making way for the \nnext fast-moving wave in the persistent northwesterly flow regime to \nmove through our area, bringing the next round of scattered showers \nand storms this afternoon and evening. The change with this update \nwill be a better chance for a few strong to severe storms, mainly \nowed to higher levels of instability expected this \nafternoon/evening, with CAPE values now forecast to be in the \n1000-1200 J/kg range and 0-6km shear remaining around 30-35 \nknots...all combined with favorable timing of the mid level \nshortwave crossing our area. Overall moisture will also get a \nmodest boost with this shortwave trough, as PWAT values climb \nback into the 1.3\" to 1.5\" range. Though not excessively high, \nthis will allow for the possibility of heavy rain to accompany \nany stronger storms, however a progressive storm movement should\nmitigate any localized flash flooding concerns. Putting this \nall together, does now appear there may be at least some threat \nfor a few strong to severe storms this afternoon/evening. Main \nthreat will be strong to damaging winds, with a lesser threat of \nlarge hail. Areas with the best potential for seeing a few strong to \nsevere storms will be from the Finger Lakes region westward where \nthe better instability will develop, and thus the SPC now has a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe weather across these areas.",
